WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:06.750
Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:08.610
My name is Tyler Morgan-Wall.

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.250
I'm the developer of rayshader
and the rayverse.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:13.250
And in the next 15
minutes, I'm going

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:15.410
to tell you how you
can turn your 3D data

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:19.860
visualizations into a theme
park ride entirely in R.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.810
Now, before we get
started, I want

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.910
you to think back to the
very first theme park

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:25.950
ride or roller coaster
you ever went on.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.560
I, for one, very clearly
remember my first roller

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:32.369
coaster ride because I was
7 and it was a disaster.

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:36.420
It was a bumpy, rough wooden
coaster that bruised my ribs

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:38.050
and rattled my spine.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:42.180
And every hill, turn, and bump
is still ingrained in my mind.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:44.250
Now, I don't have many
memories from when I was

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:48.020
seven, but that is one of them.

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:51.560
Now I want you to try to
think back to the first data

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:53.870
visualization you ever made.

00:00:53.870 --> 00:01:01.760
It was probably something like
this, this, or God forbid, this.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:04.519
But you probably don't remember
the first data visualization

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:05.883
you ever put together.

00:01:05.883 --> 00:01:08.300
And that's because we tend to
remember things in our lives

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.970
that elicit an
emotional response.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.190
My first roller
coaster ride

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:15.860
stuck with me because
of the emotions

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:18.290
it evoked, even if
they were negative.

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:19.760
Now, you might be
thinking, "well,

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.500
"data visualizations aren't
supposed to evoke emotion.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.300
They're supposed to be
about informing the reader."

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.040
And to that, I'd like
to make an analogy

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.630
to another type of
institution that

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:30.520
needs to balance
fun and interesting

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:34.630
with informative and
educational: museums.

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:36.010
I like to look at
building a data

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:39.370
visualization like a museum
curating an exhibit.

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:42.430
Museums can have a bunch
of self-contained exhibits

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:45.730
that are static, small in scope,
and designed so that viewers

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:48.580
can digest the information
at their own pace, much

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:50.680
like most data visualizations.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.350
But that doesn't mean
museums can't also

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:57.070
build a giant IMAX theater
or include a virtual reality

00:01:57.070 --> 00:02:00.460
thrill ride, something that will
evoke a feeling of excitement

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:04.000
in patrons long after the
information from the placards

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.220
has faded from their memory.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:09.699
So these are two pictures
from the Air and Space museums

00:02:09.699 --> 00:02:14.200
here in DC. They have exhibits
that both inform and entertain.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.790
And this isn't just conjecture.

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:18.850
Numerous studies have shown that
the most vivid memories tend

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:22.090
to be of emotional events, which
are recalled with more clarity

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:24.460
and detail than neutral events.

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:27.010
So while not every exhibit
should be a thrill ride, that

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:30.060
doesn't mean none can be.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.730
I'm going to show you how
you can make experiences out

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:34.680
of your data
visualizations, how you

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.790
can turn ggplot into
a heart pounding ride.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:42.090
Now, it doesn't necessarily
have to be a fast moving roller

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:43.490
coaster like this.

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:47.645
Yours might be more It's A
Small World than Space Mountain.

00:02:47.645 --> 00:02:49.770
But I want you to have this
tool in your skill set,

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:52.590
and I'm going to show you how
you don't need any fancy 3D

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:55.710
modeling skills or Pixar
level animation skills

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:58.580
to make fun 3D rides.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:01.430
So here's what you'll learn
in the next 12 minutes.

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:05.210
How to make a 3D roller
coaster in R, but why you will,

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:07.940
but also why you might want to.

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:10.310
But first, I'm going to
go over some basic ways

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:13.680
to make 3D plots in order that
don't require any fancy skills.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.970
And I'll go over what
makes a good 3D data viz.

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:18.950
And finally, the
most common feedback

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:21.500
I get when I give a talk
on 3D data visualization

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.870
is, wow, I wish I could
make something like that,

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:26.770
but I don't work
with any 3D data.

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:28.450
So throughout this
presentation, I'm

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:31.120
going to give a bunch of
examples of 3D data

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:34.120
visualizations made from a
variety of different data sets

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.850
all in R that will hopefully
give you some inspiration

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:40.270
to try and make
something of your own.

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:43.060
Now there are a few packages
out there for 3D data

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:44.290
visualization.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:46.480
But I'm going to talk
about the Rayverse,

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:48.580
the set of packages I
developed that focuses

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:50.560
on making high
quality 3D renders

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:52.510
with realistic lighting.

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:54.430
The main package I'll
be using in this process

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:56.500
is rayrender, a
package that enables

00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:00.730
you to make beautiful path trace
3D visualizations entirely

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:01.900
in R.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:03.640
The rayshader
package will be used

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:06.580
to transform your data
into 3D models using

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:09.790
either geospatial data or the
incredibly simple 3D ggplot

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:11.110
interface.

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:13.810
And finally, I wrote the
rayrollercoaster package

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:16.320
specifically for this talk.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:19.140
It automates the entire roller
coaster construction process

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:22.230
down to two functions: one
to interactively build

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:25.440
your coaster and
another to animate it.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:26.980
So let's cover the basics.

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:30.860
What exactly goes into a
3D data visualization?

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:34.400
First, what kind of data
can be used in 3D plots?

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:37.010
All you need for a 3D data viz
are data with at least

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:38.990
three numeric components.

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:41.120
Here we have European
flight path data

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:43.910
from the crowdsourced
OpenSky network.

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:47.300
Flight path data has latitude,
longitude, and altitude,

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:51.830
which intuitively correspond
to x, y and z

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:54.800
and are here
plotted as 3D paths.

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:57.830
This is a fairly straightforward
and intuitive use of 3D,

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:02.150
but you can also choose to use
3D aesthetics with 2D data,

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:04.750
much like you might choose a
line chart over a bar chart

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:06.420
purely for aesthetic reasons,

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:09.410
you might also choose a 3D
chart over its 2D counterpart

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:11.930
because you can make it more
interesting and visually

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:13.350
appealing.

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:16.380
This is a map of the submarine
fiber optic cable network,

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:18.690
and there's no actual
3D data present, just

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:20.713
latitude and longitude data.

00:05:20.713 --> 00:05:23.130
The cables rise out of the
Earth where they start and fall

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:24.990
back in where they land.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:28.350
You could easily plot this as
simple 2D paths on a world map,

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:30.720
but I think the floating
cables, subtle shadows

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:32.460
and rotating globe
really elevate

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:35.130
what is otherwise a fairly
simple data set into something

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:38.340
more relatable and tangible on
a human scale like the Earth

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:41.040
is being wrapped in yarn.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:43.560
So, many 3D plots
have--or 2D plots

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:45.480
have direct 3D
equivalents, but just

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:48.060
replacing points and lines
with spheres and cylinders

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:49.770
can lead to mixed results.

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:52.650
Data floating in the void can be
hard to interpret if we're just

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:55.523
presented with a static image
as objects in the foreground

00:05:55.523 --> 00:05:56.940
can block those
in the background,

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:00.030
as we see here in this
rendering of a morphine molecule

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:03.690
generated with
the raymolecule package.

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:08.920
But you can solve this problem
by animating the 3D data.

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:12.710
The movement disambiguates the
foreground from the background

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:17.690
so that the reader can fully
interpret the 3D model.

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:19.140
Besides points and lines,

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:21.260
the other main type of
3D data visualization

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:25.160
is the 3D mesh, a continuous
grid of data values.

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:27.980
This is where the rayshader
package comes in.

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:31.070
It takes 2D grids of raster
data and creates 3D models

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:34.340
by transforming the flat
matrix data into 3D.

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:37.430
So this visualization of the
underwater volcano, Hunga Tonga

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:39.470
that erupted earlier
this year was

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:43.190
generated from a simple 2D
matrix of bathymetry values

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:45.680
taken from a sonar survey.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:47.790
Check out the code to
see how straightforward

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:51.650
this is-- from R matrix to 3D
model in just a few function

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:53.120
calls.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:55.580
Now, this is true of almost
all of the visualizations

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:56.740
you'll see in this talk.

00:06:56.740 --> 00:06:58.250
The rendering script
is usually only

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:00.110
a dozen or so lines of code.

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:04.540
Most of the work is in cleaning
the data, as it always is.

00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:06.610
So when you're dealing
with non topographic data,

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:07.820
the process is similar.

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:09.670
You can convert the
previously 2D data

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:12.070
by extruding it into
the third dimension.

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:13.930
If you're having
trouble conceptualizing

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:16.580
what type of data can
be used for a 3D mesh.

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:18.190
One rule of thumb
is that any type

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:20.530
of data that can be mapped
to the fill or color

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:24.340
aesthetic and ggplot can
also be mapped to 3D.

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:27.160
Here we have a plot of
infections in John Snow's

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:29.530
famous cholera data
set, where the points

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:31.660
are colored by the number
of nearest neighbors

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:33.070
for each infection.

00:07:33.070 --> 00:07:35.440
The process of
converting ggplots to 3D

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.980
is fully automated in
rayshaders plot_gg() function.

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:39.730
And if you want to
learn more information,

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:43.990
check out my R RStudio conf
2020 talk on making 3D ggplots

00:07:43.990 --> 00:07:46.060
with rayshader.

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:49.690
So one reason you might consider
using 3D instead of color

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:52.300
is that encoding data to
color requires the viewer

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:54.280
to reference a color
bar to understand

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:57.460
the ordering of the data, as
there's no intuitive reason why

00:07:57.460 --> 00:07:59.410
some colors correspond
to lower values

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:01.510
and some colors
correspond to higher.

00:08:01.510 --> 00:08:03.370
Here, I plotted the
heights of buildings

00:08:03.370 --> 00:08:07.510
in Seattle, Washington, by
mapping the data to color.

00:08:07.510 --> 00:08:10.150
Height, on the other
hand, intuitively

00:08:10.150 --> 00:08:12.760
allows you to judge which
values are less than or greater

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:13.815
than others.

00:08:13.815 --> 00:08:15.190
You can tell when
a building here

00:08:15.190 --> 00:08:17.140
is about half the
height of another

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:20.440
without having to reference
an external color bar.

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:24.280
However, because 3D introduces
ambiguity in the depth axis,

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:25.700
you lose precision.

00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:28.480
Thus, 3D data visualizations
are great when

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:30.490
the exact numeric
values aren't important,

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:33.130
but comparing relative values
and getting an overall sense

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:35.780
of the distribution of data is.

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:40.309
Using both 3D and color can give
you the best of both worlds.

00:08:40.309 --> 00:08:42.919
Color to indicate precise
numeric levels and height

00:08:42.919 --> 00:08:45.770
to give a more intuitive
way to compare values.

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:48.590
Here we have a visualization I
made of the global population

00:08:48.590 --> 00:08:51.170
density using both
color and height.

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:53.510
The use of color allows
the viewer to map data

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:56.270
to direct population intervals,
while the use of height

00:08:56.270 --> 00:09:00.600
establishes order without
needing to reference a legend.

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:02.820
Now, if you're the
type of person like me

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:06.000
that likes to obsess over the
tiniest details of your data viz,

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:07.430
good news!

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:09.660
3D brings an entirely
new type of detail

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:11.490
to obsess about: lighting.

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:15.560
Move over, adjusting
ggplot margins.

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:17.630
If you've ever talked
to a photographer,

00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:19.820
you know the real secret
to amazing photographs

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:22.490
isn't necessarily having
the best equipment,

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:26.140
but rather having adequate
and well designed lighting.

00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:28.420
If you only have a single
light like we do here

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:32.710
in this 3D historical map, this
visualization is a bit spooky.

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:36.060
Like a swing light bulb
in a haunted house.

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:38.790
If you look at this
visualization of the blooming

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:42.240
California poppy fields, we
see--with multiple distant fill

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:45.270
lights, we see the atmosphere
has changed completely.

00:09:45.270 --> 00:09:48.060
Much less moody
and much brighter.

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:51.510
Even easier than all of that
is using an HDR image file

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:53.310
for environment
based lighting, which

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:55.980
we see here in this
visualization of Monterey

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:57.630
Bay, California.

00:09:57.630 --> 00:09:59.610
It lights the scene
with a real world image

00:09:59.610 --> 00:10:01.860
which results in natural,
realistic lighting

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:05.330
like your scene was
in that environment.

00:10:05.330 --> 00:10:07.280
If your data changes
over time, you

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:09.810
can then also introduce
animation to your data viz.

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:13.730
Here, I used a Microsoft
Kinect to capture my movements

00:10:13.730 --> 00:10:18.120
and load them into R
using the RMoCap package.

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:21.210
By extracting the individual
time slices from the movement

00:10:21.210 --> 00:10:23.710
data and rendering
each frame completely,

00:10:23.710 --> 00:10:26.580
I was able to combine all the
frames into this animation

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:28.240
using the av package.

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:29.770
So watch out Pixar,

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:33.400
R users are coming for you.
[laughter]

00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:37.600
You can even use this ability
to bring old friends back

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:38.250
from the dead. [applause]

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:41.810
Thank you.

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:42.340
Thank you.

00:10:42.340 --> 00:10:44.290
Thank you.

00:10:44.290 --> 00:10:46.720
So here I've used
R and rayrender

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:49.570
to bring Microsoft
Clippy back to life

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:53.020
to help you debug your
data analysis pipeline.

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:56.310
Maybe an idea for Posit's next
professional product.

00:10:56.310 --> 00:11:01.160
[laughter]
And for the creatively inclined,

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:04.000
you can also produce
stunning generative art in 3D

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:05.470
using rayrender.

00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:08.470
Here, I've simulated and plotted
the trajectory of a particle

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:11.410
in a strange attractor, a
type of differential equation

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:13.300
that displays chaotic motion.

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:15.580
I've lit the scene with
both a white and red light

00:11:15.580 --> 00:11:17.170
and changed the
material of the object

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:18.850
to give it a glossy sheen.

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:20.890
We can also generate
fractal trees

00:11:20.890 --> 00:11:22.640
using the raybonsai package.

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:25.840
This is a rewrite of Danielle
Navarro's flametree package

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:28.390
to work in 3D, and you can
create all different types

00:11:28.390 --> 00:11:31.030
of trees with two or
three lines of code

00:11:31.030 --> 00:11:34.690
all generated in R
with nothing but math.

00:11:34.690 --> 00:11:37.480
And finally, unlike in 2D,
where there's only one way

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:39.280
to look at a
visualization, in 3D,

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:41.560
you have an infinite
number of camera angles,

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:43.920
orientations, and
settings to choose from.

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:46.180
It can be intimidating,
especially if you've never

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:50.230
worked in 3D before, but I've
made it a little less daunting

00:11:50.230 --> 00:11:53.590
by including a fully
interactive, real time 3D to

00:11:53.590 --> 00:11:55.810
graphics device
built into rayrender

00:11:55.810 --> 00:11:58.540
and working out of the
box with no dependencies,

00:11:58.540 --> 00:12:00.640
or no weird dependencies.

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:03.220
You can fly around your
scene with just your mouse

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:04.990
and keyboard and
find the best angle

00:12:04.990 --> 00:12:08.080
and print out your camera
info for future use.

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:11.570
This is all happening
directly in your R session.

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:14.540
You can even interactively save
keyframes and then generate

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:18.200
an animation smoothly flying
through your data visualization

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:22.380
or, as I'm about to show
you, riding through it.

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:26.070
Now that you know everything you need
to know to make a 3D data viz in R,

00:12:26.070 --> 00:12:29.310
let's build a roller coaster.

00:12:29.310 --> 00:12:32.310
First, we need to generate a
3D scene for our roller coaster

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:33.250
to travel through.

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:36.060
Now, this could be any of the
visualizations I've shown up

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:40.860
to this point, but I'm going to
use a 3D ggplot because rayshader's

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:43.140
3D ggplot interface is
by far the easiest

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:47.530
way to make a slick
3D data viz in R.

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:49.690
This is a density plot
of the diamonds data

00:12:49.690 --> 00:12:53.140
set in R with the fill
aesthetic mapped to 3D.

00:12:53.140 --> 00:12:56.890
This is one function call,
passing the ggplot object, and voila!

00:12:56.890 --> 00:12:59.380
A fully formed 3D data viz.

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:02.260
You might note that I've turned
off rayshader's ray tracing

00:13:02.260 --> 00:13:04.390
because we're going to
get a much higher quality

00:13:04.390 --> 00:13:06.340
version of ray
tracing when we render

00:13:06.340 --> 00:13:08.350
the scene with rayrender.

00:13:08.350 --> 00:13:11.200
Now that we have
our 3D scene set up,

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:14.150
here's where the rayrollercoaster
package comes in.

00:13:14.150 --> 00:13:17.140
Here, all we need to do is call
the generate_rayshader_coaster()

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:19.750
function, which extracts
the rayshader scene for you

00:13:19.750 --> 00:13:23.080
and automates the entire roller
coaster construction process.

00:13:23.080 --> 00:13:25.570
This function uses rayshaders--
or rayrenders

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:27.370
built in interactive
graphics device

00:13:27.370 --> 00:13:29.710
to let you fly around the
scene and build a roller

00:13:29.710 --> 00:13:31.240
coaster directly in R.

00:13:31.240 --> 00:13:33.730
Here, I've recorded
a video of myself

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:36.130
flying through the
scene pressing k

00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:37.400
at the key positions

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:40.870
I want my roller coaster to
travel through. When I'm done

00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:42.390
and close the window,

00:13:42.390 --> 00:13:45.340
it will generate a 3D
model of a roller coaster

00:13:45.340 --> 00:13:47.590
that travels through
those points.

00:13:47.590 --> 00:13:50.590
Now I've extracted the full 3D
scene here with get_rayrender_scene()

00:13:50.590 --> 00:13:54.190
and used render_scene() to
get an overhead isometric view

00:13:54.190 --> 00:13:55.170
of the ride.

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:57.340
Sort of like Roller
Coaster Tycoon,

00:13:57.340 --> 00:14:00.300
but with less death
and destruction.

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:02.440
Now that we're happy
with our roller coaster,

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:06.150
all that's left is simply
calling animate_rollercoaster()

00:14:06.150 --> 00:14:11.040
with a movie file name, and
we get our full first-person

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:14.670
animated roller coaster ride
through our data set entirely

00:14:14.670 --> 00:14:24.890
in R. Ta da!
[applause]

00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:28.830
So we're all done creating
our roller coaster.

00:14:28.830 --> 00:14:30.640
Or are we?

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:33.010
You see, while this is
cool, you might note

00:14:33.010 --> 00:14:35.580
that it's more ride than data viz.

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:37.580
You can't look around
because the field of view

00:14:37.580 --> 00:14:40.450
here is fixed at 120
degrees,

00:14:40.450 --> 00:14:43.240
and the camera view is
stuck along the track.

00:14:43.240 --> 00:14:47.470
If instead, we set the field
of view to 360 degrees,

00:14:47.470 --> 00:14:50.260
we can render the entire scene
at every frame, which gives us

00:14:50.260 --> 00:14:53.150
something that looks like this.

00:14:53.150 --> 00:14:56.000
So this is rendering
a complete sphere

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:57.710
surrounding the
camera at every frame

00:14:57.710 --> 00:15:00.080
so that we can see
every angle at once.

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:03.140
This allows the viewer
to choose where to look.

00:15:03.140 --> 00:15:07.820
Now, this animation looks
rather funky and warped,

00:15:07.820 --> 00:15:10.820
but we aren't going to
use the video in this form

00:15:10.820 --> 00:15:12.800
because you can do
something very special

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:15.980
with 360 degree
videos by injecting

00:15:15.980 --> 00:15:19.910
a little metadata to the mp4
file and uploading to YouTube,

00:15:19.910 --> 00:15:24.740
You can turn this video into
a virtual reality 3D roller coaster

00:15:24.740 --> 00:15:35.200
and put yourself directly
inside your data viz. [applause]

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:37.930
Now, the viewer can look
and direct their attention

00:15:37.930 --> 00:15:40.900
to wherever they want as
they fly along the track.

00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:42.520
Because you control
where you look,

00:15:42.520 --> 00:15:44.530
it truly feels like
you're riding a thrill

00:15:44.530 --> 00:15:47.380
ride with exciting,
vertigo inducing drops

00:15:47.380 --> 00:15:48.680
and dizzying turns

00:15:48.680 --> 00:15:50.970
that truly leaves an impression.

00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:53.700
You don't need any fancy
VR gear to experience this.

00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:57.870
I made this video with just my
phone and a $20 VR head mount,

00:15:57.870 --> 00:16:00.420
and it only took a dozen
or so lines of code

00:16:00.420 --> 00:16:05.000
to put together thanks to the
power of R and the rayverse.

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:07.850
So in summary, 3D plotting
is an awesome tool

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:11.300
you should consider trying out,
and there's no better ecosystem

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:14.360
out there for 3D
data viz than R.

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:16.700
Thanks to rayrender's
interactive render and the

00:16:16.700 --> 00:16:17.790
rayrollercoaster package,

00:16:17.790 --> 00:16:20.150
building an impressive thrill
ride through your 3D world

00:16:20.150 --> 00:16:23.430
is dead simple and only
takes a few lines of code.

00:16:23.430 --> 00:16:26.540
And finally, consider combining
the tools of virtual reality

00:16:26.540 --> 00:16:29.370
with 3D data viz to immerse
your viewers on rides or tours

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:30.470
throughout your data.

00:16:30.470 --> 00:16:33.170
By actually putting your viewers
in the data visualization,

00:16:33.170 --> 00:16:35.510
you can form longer lasting
connections to your work

00:16:35.510 --> 00:16:37.640
by evoking emotion
and allowing the user

00:16:37.640 --> 00:16:40.610
to experience your visualization
in a more up close

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:42.230
and personal way.

00:16:42.230 --> 00:16:45.790
Thank you so much for your
time, and enjoy the ride!

